WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.951
Idea. Think of your favourite crisps. Every year or so the brand brings out a new flavour. Why? Because people get bored, and a new flavour gives them a reason to buy again.

00:00:11.550 --> 00:00:19.217
A new product is one part of the marketing mix. The mix is the four Ps: product, price, place and promotion.

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:31.735
A product can be a good or a service. A good is a physical item you can touch, such as a car. A service is an activity done for you, such as washing a car or giving advice.

00:00:32.850 --> 00:00:47.931
Method. Businesses keep introducing new products to keep customers interested, keep up with trends, and replace products that are declining. A new feature can also give a unique selling point: something that makes it different from rivals.

00:00:48.500 --> 00:01:03.282
Step one: when a case gives options, say which part of the mix each one changes. A new drink changes the product. Half-price Mondays change the price. Selling online changes the place. A radio advert is promotion.

00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:14.065
Step two: link the option to customers: who it attracts, and why they would buy. Then carry the chain to revenue or market share, using a fact from the case.

00:01:14.650 --> 00:01:22.840
Step three: give a drawback. New products cost money to develop and may fail, so research and careful planning reduce the risk.

00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:36.919
Worked example. Here is a real question from Paper two R in November 2020. Explain one benefit to a business of continually introducing new products. It is worth three marks.

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:42.564
Mark one is the benefit: new products keep customers interested in the business.

00:01:43.150 --> 00:01:53.229
Mark two is the link. When older products go out of date, or customers get bored, a new one gives them a reason to come back instead of switching to a competitor.

00:01:53.800 --> 00:02:00.667
Mark three is the result. The business keeps up with changing trends, so its sales are maintained over time.

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:08.716
Common mistake. Mistake one: calling a service a good. Gardening is done for you, so it is a service.

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:17.406
Mistake two: adding profit or production to the marketing mix. The four Ps are product, price, place and promotion.

00:02:18.550 --> 00:02:35.521
Your turn. Now you try. A café's sales are falling. Option A is to add a new range of smoothies. Option B is to cut all prices by ten per cent. Which part of the mix does each option change? Give one drawback of option A. Pause and decide.

00:02:36.650 --> 00:02:50.851
Answer. Option A changes the product. Option B changes the price. A drawback of option A: the café must pay for blenders and fresh fruit, and if customers do not like the smoothies, that money is lost.

00:02:51.450 --> 00:03:00.877
That is the method for this type: name the part of the mix, link it to customers and sales, then weigh the drawback. Now try the practice questions.
